### Part 2: Comprehensive Mimikatz YARA Rule

This rule focuses on **static binary analysis** (detecting files or strings in memory).
*   **Limitation:** Mimikatz is notorious for obfuscation and renaming. This rule detects "Classic" and "Modern" (Post-2020) variants. It will not necessarily catch every renamed binary (e.g., if someone renames `mimikatz.exe` to `svchost.exe`), but it will catch those retaining original signatures.
*   **Best Practice:** YARA rules are for *triage*. Do not rely solely on static signatures for active blocking.

```yara
rule Mimikatz_Detection_Variant 
{
    meta:
        author      = "Your_Name_Security"
        date        = "2023-10-27"
        description = "Detects Mimikatz tools and common credential dumping commands"
        score       = 90
        categories  = "Credential_Access", "Malware_Signature", "Post_Exploitation"
        severity    = "High"
        yara_version = "4.3"

    condition:
        // Group 1: Exact File Name Checks (Commonly detected)
        filesize < 2 MB and md5(sha256_hash) in ("...add_specific_hashes_if_known...") or

        // Group 2: Common Command Strings (The most robust method)
        // Note: These commands are often stripped by modern mimikatz versions,
        // so we use full ascii and hex patterns where applicable.

        /* 
           Classic Mimikatz Strings 
           These are the core functionality calls that persist across versions.
        */

        // Main Executable or Library
        strings:
            $exe_name1 = { 6D 69 6D 69 6B 61 74 7A } ; // "mimikatz" hex encoded
            $exe_name2 = "mimikatz.exe" ;
            $exe_name3 = "mimikatz" ;

            // Command line arguments (ASCII)
            $cmd_dump_pass = "sekurlsa::logonpasswords" ;
            $cmd_secrets = "sekurlsa::secrets" ;
