Yes, in C++, you can atomically compare and exchange an atomic integer `i` with a boundary value `x`, incrementing `i` only if `i < x`. This must be done carefully to avoid race conditions when multiple threads may attempt the same operation concurrently.

### Core Idea

Use a loop with `std::atomic::compare_exchange_weak()` or `compare_exchange_strong()`:

- Load the current value of the atomic variable `i`
- Check if it's less than `x`
- Attempt to exchange it with the incremented value
- If the exchange fails due to concurrent modification, reload the new value and retry

### Example Implementation (C++17 and later)

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <iostream>

void atomic_increment_if_below(int& i_value, int x) {
    std::atomic<int>* ptr = &i_value;
    std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_release);

    while (true) {
        int current = ptr->load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (current >= x) break;  // Boundary reached

        int next = current + 1;
        if (ptr->compare_exchange_weak(
                current,
                next,
                std::memory_order_acq_rel,
                std::memory_order_acquire)) {
            break;  // Exchange succeeded
        }
        // Compare-and-swap failed; another thread changed value
    }
}
```

### Key Considerations

- Use `weak` version (`compare_exchange_weak`) for better performance on most platforms, but be prepared to retry on failure.
- Ensure appropriate memory ordering (`acquire`, `release`, `acq_rel`) depending on your synchronization requirements.
- This guarantees that `i` never exceeds `x`, even under concurrent access from multiple threads.
- If you just need to ensure the maximum value is not exceeded without modifying it further after reaching `x`, consider adding checks in other threads to avoid overflow.

This pattern is known as a "CAS-based bounded increment", and is commonly used to implement thread-safe bounded counters and semaphores.

